## **What is Wireless Networking?**

Wireless networking, at its most fundamental level, is the transmission of data through the air using **radio waves** instead of physical cables. This convenience — the ability to connect without wires — is also the source of its greatest security challenge.

With a wired Ethernet network, an attacker needs **physical access** to plug into a cable or network port. With wireless, the signal travels through walls, floors, and even outside the building. Anyone within range of your wireless signal can attempt to interact with your network — and you often don't even know they're there.

This single fact — **the medium is the air, and air is shared by everyone** — drives almost every wireless security concern and solution we'll discuss.

The dominant wireless networking standard today is **Wi-Fi**, which is based on the **IEEE 802.11** family of standards. When people say "wireless security" in a networking context, they almost always mean 802.11 Wi-Fi security.

---

## **How Wi-Fi Works — The Fundamentals**

### **Basic Architecture**

**Access Point (AP):**\
The central device in a wireless network. It's connected to the wired network and broadcasts a wireless signal that client devices connect to. Think of it as the wireless equivalent of a switch.

**Station (STA):**\
Any client device that connects to the wireless network — laptops, phones, tablets, IoT devices.

**BSS (Basic Service Set):**\
The basic building block of a wireless network — one access point and all the stations connected to it. Each BSS is identified by a **BSSID**, which is the MAC address of the access point.

**SSID (Service Set Identifier):**\
The human-readable name of the wireless network — "HomeNetwork," "CoffeeShop_WiFi," etc. The AP broadcasts this name in **beacon frames** so devices can discover it.

**ESS (Extended Service Set):**\
Multiple BSSs (multiple access points) connected together to form one larger network with seamless roaming. All APs in an ESS share the same SSID. Large university or corporate wireless networks are ESS configurations.

### **Frequency Bands and Channels**

Wi-Fi operates on specific **radio frequency bands:**

**2.4 GHz band:**

- Longer range, better penetration through walls
- More congested — many devices use this band (microwaves, Bluetooth, baby monitors, neighbors' Wi-Fi)
- Fewer non-overlapping channels (only 3 in most regions: channels 1, 6, and 11)
- Older and slower in maximum speeds

**5 GHz band:**

- Shorter range, less wall penetration
- Much less congested
- More non-overlapping channels
- Higher speeds
- Used by newer 802.11 standards (802.11a, 802.11n dual-band, 802.11ac, 802.11ax)

**6 GHz band:**

- Newest band, introduced with Wi-Fi 6E (802.11ax extended)
- Even more channels, very low congestion currently
- Even shorter range

The channel you operate on matters for security — attackers can tune to the same channel and frequency to capture your traffic.

